---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import LtvCacCalculator from '../../components/LtvCacCalculator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="LTV:CAC Calculator" description="Calculate customer lifetime value, customer acquisition cost, and the LTV:CAC ratio investors look at.">
  <LtvCacCalculator client:load slot="tool" />
  <h2>What is a Good LTV:CAC Ratio?</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Ratio</th><th>What it means</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Below 1:1</td><td>Losing money on every customer acquired</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1:1 – 2:1</td><td>Unsustainable long-term without other efficiencies</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3:1</td><td>Commonly cited healthy benchmark</td></tr>
      <tr><td>5:1+</td><td>Very efficient — may even signal under-investing in growth</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>A common benchmark is that LTV should be at least 3x CAC — high enough to cover acquisition cost, support, and still leave healthy margin. But this varies by industry and stage; a very high ratio can also mean you're leaving growth on the table by under-spending on acquisition.</p>

  <h2>Common LTV:CAC Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Treating 3:1 as a universal target</strong> regardless of industry or business model — the "right" ratio varies by margin structure and growth stage.</li>
    <li><strong>Calculating LTV and CAC over mismatched time periods</strong>, comparing numbers that aren't actually apples-to-apples.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This LTV:CAC Calculator</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Evaluating whether current acquisition spend is sustainable long-term</li>
    <li>Reporting unit economics health to investors or leadership</li>
    <li>Comparing LTV:CAC across different marketing channels</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>LTV:CAC Ratio FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>What is the ideal LTV to CAC ratio?</strong> 3:1 is the most commonly cited benchmark, though the right target varies by industry, margin structure, and growth stage — earlier-stage companies sometimes accept a lower ratio to prioritize growth.</p>
  <p><strong>How do I calculate LTV to CAC?</strong> Divide your customer lifetime value by your customer acquisition cost. Enter both figures above and the calculator returns the ratio directly.</p>
</ToolLayout>
